<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><channel><language>en</language><title>Environment news and headlines - RFI</title><description>Follow all Environment news on RFI.</description><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/</link><lastBuildDate>Fri, 28 Aug 2026 05:29:34 GMT</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/rss" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><category>Environment</category><title>Cop17 races for desertification deal as report warns half world’s soils under threat</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20260828-cop17-soil-governance-essential-to-global-deal-on-combatting-drought</link><description>
Delegates at the Cop17 summit on desertification in Mongolia are seeking to seal a deal to combat drought on Friday, the last day of the event – as a United Nations report warns the world's soils are rapidly deteriorating, while tried and tested solutions are not being implemented. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/4ff49786-7382-11f1-9445-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/000-B8EN6N4.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/4ff49786-7382-11f1-9445-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/000-B8EN6N4.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">28015e30-a226-11f1-84a1-f7f1b19f1d48</guid><pubDate>Fri, 28 Aug 2026 05:29:03 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/4ff49786-7382-11f1-9445-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/000-B8EN6N4.jpg">© STEFANO RELLANDINI / AFP</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Environment</category><title>France says US risks ‘blocking everything’ at UN talks to protect world’s soils</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20260825-france-says-us-risks-blocking-everything-at-un-talks-to-protect-world-soils-cop17-desertification</link><description>
The soil that grows the world's food and holds its water is becoming less productive, yet governments are struggling to agree on how to respond to this crisis. As political talks at Cop17, the United Nations conference on desertification and land degradation, stepped up in Mongolia on Tuesday, France's environment ambassador Barbara Pompili warned that disputes involving the United States are threatening progress. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/25db6fc2-a098-11f1-a9d6-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP22305010588451.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/25db6fc2-a098-11f1-a9d6-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP22305010588451.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">2868478e-a097-11f1-9bbb-23fdf51191ba</guid><pubDate>Tue, 25 Aug 2026 15:59:31 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/25db6fc2-a098-11f1-a9d6-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP22305010588451.jpg">AP - Rafiq Maqbool</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Environment</category><title>How degradation and drought are threatening the land that feeds us</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20260819-degradation-and-drought-threatening-the-land-that-feeds-us-cop17-desrtification-jean-luc-chotte</link><description>
Desertification is not the spread of deserts. It is the slow degradation of fertile land – a process that now affects 40 percent of the Earth's land surface and leaves around 100 million hectares of healthy land unproductive every year. As governments gather in Mongolia this week for the UN's Cop17 desertification summit, scientists say restoring soil health is becoming critical for food security, livelihoods and climate resilience. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/53633cb8-8eaa-11f1-b197-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/Photo-Hien-1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/53633cb8-8eaa-11f1-b197-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/Photo-Hien-1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">7b4a3168-9ae0-11f1-8667-d5a741bcbce0</guid><pubDate>Wed, 19 Aug 2026 10:55:07 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/53633cb8-8eaa-11f1-b197-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/Photo-Hien-1.jpg">© Edmond Hien/IRD</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Environment</category><title>Cop17 aims to restore land and hope in fight against desertification</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20260817-cop17-aims-to-restore-land-and-hope-in-fight-against-desertification</link><description>
Extreme weather across much of Europe and elsewhere is lending added weight to the United Nations Convention to Combat Desertification (Cop17), which begins on Monday in Mongolia. RFI looks at what's at stake worldwide. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/614acb7c-3108-11ef-b594-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/000_33V82Y9.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/614acb7c-3108-11ef-b594-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/000_33V82Y9.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">5c1b8fec-9a1f-11f1-820b-111cad60ec80</guid><pubDate>Mon, 17 Aug 2026 10:52:37 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/614acb7c-3108-11ef-b594-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/000_33V82Y9.jpg">© Karel Prinsloo / AFP</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Environment</category><title>Europe's shrinking rivers give rise to debate over how to tackle drought</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20260816-europe-s-shrinking-rivers-give-rise-to-debate-over-how-to-tackle-drought</link><description>
Europe's shrinking rivers are disrupting industry, reducing power generation and intensifying the debate over how to respond to worsening drought. As water levels fall across the continent, authorities and businesses alike are looking for ways to keep freight moving and critical infrastructure operating. Hydrologists warn, however, that digging rivers deeper cannot create more water and could make shortages worse.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/501dd132-9056-11f1-aee0-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26209461327444.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/501dd132-9056-11f1-aee0-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26209461327444.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">6b280094-97ca-11f1-90af-371e64013e18</guid><pubDate>Sun, 16 Aug 2026 07:17:26 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/501dd132-9056-11f1-aee0-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26209461327444.jpg">© Federico Gambarini/DPA via AP</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Environment</category><title>UN urges faster action as world falls behind on nature protection</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/un-urges-faster-action-as-world-falls-behind-on-nature-protection</link><description>
The world is falling behind on its commitments to protect nature, the UN’s biodiversity chief has warned, ahead of a major international conference in Armenia aimed at accelerating action.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/51d866ee-5b38-11f0-9c19-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/poisson-mort-mediterranee-carrefour-maghreb-ok.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/51d866ee-5b38-11f0-9c19-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/poisson-mort-mediterranee-carrefour-maghreb-ok.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">ff6a124a-971d-11f1-a89e-815d44e28883</guid><pubDate>Fri, 14 Aug 2026 06:12:12 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/51d866ee-5b38-11f0-9c19-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/poisson-mort-mediterranee-carrefour-maghreb-ok.jpg">© AP Photo/Ariel Schalit</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Environment</category><title>Romania shuts down nuclear plant due to low level of drought-hit Danube</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20260813-romania-shuts-down-nuclear-plant-due-to-low-level-of-drought-hit-danube</link><description>
Romania on Thursday began a complete shutdown of its only nuclear plant after levels of water in the River Danube – used to cool the site – fell to a record low, the national nuclear energy company said. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/ed108bc2-96fc-11f1-89c1-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26219541763429.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/ed108bc2-96fc-11f1-89c1-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26219541763429.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">7fa3daf8-96fc-11f1-b324-8fa549d9c748</guid><pubDate>Thu, 13 Aug 2026 13:28:12 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/ed108bc2-96fc-11f1-89c1-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26219541763429.jpg">AP Photo/Andreea Alexandru - Andreea Alexandru</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Environment</category><title>Millions gather to watch as solar eclipse plunges swathe of Spain into darkness</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20260813-as-millions-gazed-in-awe-a-swathe-of-spain-plunged-into-darkness</link><description>
Millions of spectators – tourists and locals alike – gathered in parks and cities across Spain to see Wednesday's total solar eclipse, the first of its kind in the country in more than a century.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/5567e238-96df-11f1-8aa3-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Observator-astrofizic-Arcos-de-las-Salinas-Spania.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/5567e238-96df-11f1-8aa3-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Observator-astrofizic-Arcos-de-las-Salinas-Spania.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">439102cc-96e1-11f1-8094-3395aad33fd5</guid><pubDate>Thu, 13 Aug 2026 06:39:58 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/5567e238-96df-11f1-8aa3-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/Observator-astrofizic-Arcos-de-las-Salinas-Spania.jpg">© REUTERS - Christian Hartmann</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Environment</category><title>As the planet heats up, butterflies are seeking out cooler climes</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20260809-as-the-planet-heats-up-butterflies-are-seeking-out-cooler-climes</link><description>
Climate change is driving shifts in where butterflies are found globally, according to a major new study, with some species moving northwards or seeking out higher elevations in search of cooler conditions.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/0ab62654-ab7f-11ed-bf7b-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/000_8QJ9J8.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/0ab62654-ab7f-11ed-bf7b-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/000_8QJ9J8.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">5a42d016-90df-11f1-86ef-872dfaccfb68</guid><pubDate>Sun, 09 Aug 2026 13:39:45 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/0ab62654-ab7f-11ed-bf7b-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/000_8QJ9J8.jpg">© AFP - ANDREW BLADON / OXFORD UNIVERSITY</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Environment</category><title>Think this summer is hot? El Nino could push temperatures even higher</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/think-this-summer-is-hot-el-nino-could-push-temperatures-even-higher-climate-change</link><description>
Record ocean heat, intensifying El Nino conditions and worsening extremes are raising the prospect that 2026 could become the hottest year ever measured.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/05489a30-8fce-11f1-90fa-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-08-04T050053Z-269844662-RC2BPMATL017-RTRMADP-3-EUROPE-WEATHER-INSURANCE.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/05489a30-8fce-11f1-90fa-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-08-04T050053Z-269844662-RC2BPMATL017-RTRMADP-3-EUROPE-WEATHER-INSURANCE.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">d33458f0-918e-11f1-9d67-e135b2d0c4c5</guid><pubDate>Sat, 08 Aug 2026 13:16:52 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/05489a30-8fce-11f1-90fa-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-08-04T050053Z-269844662-RC2BPMATL017-RTRMADP-3-EUROPE-WEATHER-INSURANCE.jpg">© REUTERS - Manon Cruz </source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Environment</category><title>Heatwaves to increase 'fivefold by 2050', warns French weather bureau chief</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20260802-heatwaves-to-increase-fivefold-by-2050-warns-french-weather-bureau-chief</link><description>
"We have had more heatwaves in the last 15 years than during the previous 60 years," the CEO of France's weather bureau Météo-France, Virginie Schwarz said on Thursday. As France experiences its fourth heatwave of the summer, experts warn preventative measures to deal with the changing climate are severely lacking. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/041ec91c-8c0f-11f1-abb4-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-29T173908Z-1838555039-RC2SNMA1KHAO-RTRMADP-3-EUROPE-WEATHER-FRANCE-WILDFIRE.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/041ec91c-8c0f-11f1-abb4-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-29T173908Z-1838555039-RC2SNMA1KHAO-RTRMADP-3-EUROPE-WEATHER-FRANCE-WILDFIRE.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">bd7a2b72-8c05-11f1-9d12-e58b7addb2db</guid><pubDate>Sun, 02 Aug 2026 09:37:03 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/041ec91c-8c0f-11f1-abb4-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-29T173908Z-1838555039-RC2SNMA1KHAO-RTRMADP-3-EUROPE-WEATHER-FRANCE-WILDFIRE.jpg">REUTERS - Yves Herman</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Environment</category><title>Climate change 'exacerbating' drought across Europe, scientists say</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20260725-climate-change-exacerbating-drought-across-europe-scientists-say</link><description>
Climate change is exacerbating drought in Europe as extreme heat evaporates water supplies, scientists said in a study published this week, with low water levels disrupting shipping, farming and industry in countries including Romania and Greece.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/33dfd932-8437-11f1-a066-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-20T071512Z-1618978233-RC28HMARUIOU-RTRMADP-3-EUROPE-WEATHER-ROMANIA-DROUGHT.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/33dfd932-8437-11f1-a066-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-20T071512Z-1618978233-RC28HMARUIOU-RTRMADP-3-EUROPE-WEATHER-ROMANIA-DROUGHT.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">b5aee396-8675-11f1-8500-07980066211b</guid><pubDate>Sat, 25 Jul 2026 08:58:15 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/33dfd932-8437-11f1-a066-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-20T071512Z-1618978233-RC28HMARUIOU-RTRMADP-3-EUROPE-WEATHER-ROMANIA-DROUGHT.jpg">© Inquam Photos / Reuters</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Environment</category><title>Wildfire scorches 12,000 hectares north of Madrid, say Spanish authorities</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20260719-wildfire-scorches-12-000-hectares-north-of-madrid-say-spanish-authorities</link><description>
A wildfire raging 100 kilometres north of Madrid has spread, burning more than 12,000 hectares and forcing several hundred people to evacuate, local authorities announced on Sunday.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/05b17c3c-8388-11f1-83f9-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/spain-fire-000-C2J48NR.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/05b17c3c-8388-11f1-83f9-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/spain-fire-000-C2J48NR.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">7f1c39ea-8388-11f1-9197-8dedb811771e</guid><pubDate>Sun, 19 Jul 2026 15:50:15 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/05b17c3c-8388-11f1-83f9-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/spain-fire-000-C2J48NR.jpg">AFP - HANDOUT</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Environment</category><title>What can France learn from Spain's heatwave protection for workers?</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20260703-what-can-france-learn-from-spain-s-heatwave-protection-for-workers</link><description>
As French workers face record-breaking temperatures, attention is turning to Spain, where measures introduced to deal with extreme weather are inspiring a proposal for climate leave in France.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/7350b256-80e8-11f0-b2fc-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25188844289438.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/7350b256-80e8-11f0-b2fc-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25188844289438.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">00e04ece-7495-11f1-9ee9-576ed39421ff</guid><pubDate>Fri, 03 Jul 2026 05:37:26 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/7350b256-80e8-11f0-b2fc-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25188844289438.jpg">AP - Paul White</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Environment</category><title>El Nino set to be 'strong' as UN warns of heatwaves and floods</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20260703-el-nino-set-to-be-strong-as-un-warns-of-heatwaves-and-floods</link><description>
El Nino is here and will quickly develop into a strong event between July and September, fuelling the likelihood of extreme weather, the United Nations' climate agency warned Friday.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/41dc8bea-5e7e-11f1-93ef-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/38191d18f1b854ef1546f59b783b0e6e5c1dfc70.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/41dc8bea-5e7e-11f1-93ef-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/38191d18f1b854ef1546f59b783b0e6e5c1dfc70.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4576b644-7694-11f1-b4d9-554940d89b4f</guid><pubDate>Fri, 03 Jul 2026 04:04:20 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/41dc8bea-5e7e-11f1-93ef-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/38191d18f1b854ef1546f59b783b0e6e5c1dfc70.jpg">© MARTIN BERNETTI / AFP</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Environment</category><title>World Bank drops climate funding target, raising fears for Africa</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20260702-world-bank-drops-climate-funding-target-raising-fears-for-africa</link><description>
The World Bank has abandoned its target of directing 45 percent of its financing to projects that help tackle climate change, prompting warnings that poorer countries – especially in Africa – could receive less support to adapt to a warming world.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/16c63082-95a9-11ee-894a-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/AP23341508788290.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/16c63082-95a9-11ee-894a-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/AP23341508788290.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">b91adfd8-7538-11f1-9bc1-c774f26789d8</guid><pubDate>Thu, 02 Jul 2026 10:43:10 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/16c63082-95a9-11ee-894a-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/AP23341508788290.jpg">AP</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Environment</category><title>World's oceans record hottest June ever as new El Nino takes hold</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20260701-world-s-oceans-record-hottest-june-ever-as-new-el-nino-takes-hold</link><description>
The world's oceans reached their hottest June on record, scientists said on Wednesday, with sea temperatures expected to climb further as El Nino develops alongside human-driven climate change.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/475bfbf0-386a-11ea-a7a7-005056bfd1d9/w:1024/p:16x9/2017071818175613711.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/475bfbf0-386a-11ea-a7a7-005056bfd1d9/w:1024/p:16x9/2017071818175613711.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">dd3ca334-751e-11f1-8dac-1bff2aa3277d</guid><pubDate>Wed, 01 Jul 2026 07:31:23 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/475bfbf0-386a-11ea-a7a7-005056bfd1d9/w:1024/p:16x9/2017071818175613711.jpg">Flickr / Creative Commons</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Environment</category><title>Why Germany is turning to Algeria for Europe's hydrogen future</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20260628-why-germany-is-turning-to-algeria-for-europe-s-hydrogen-future</link><description>
Germany is strengthening its energy partnership with Algeria, as Europe searches for new long-term energy supplies and invests in renewable hydrogen to help cut emissions.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/85185c64-6e54-11f1-8468-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/000-32H3982.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/85185c64-6e54-11f1-8468-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/000-32H3982.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">d3ec6f92-6e4f-11f1-b7de-2b2767513eca</guid><pubDate>Sun, 28 Jun 2026 10:25:22 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/85185c64-6e54-11f1-8468-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/000-32H3982.jpg">AFP - LUDOVIC MARIN</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Environment</category><title>Marine biobanks: the science of 'stopping time' for an ecosystem under pressure</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20260627-marine-biobanks-the-science-of-stopping-time-for-an-ocean-under-pressure</link><description>
In labs around the world, the ocean is being kept in jars. Some of it is alive, bubbling in flasks, while some lies frozen in liquid nitrogen. They're marine biobanks – collections of coral cells, algae, bacteria and other living materials that scientists are working to preserve before warming seas and disappearing habitats consume them.
